# The cache and the per-tick records are regenerable and large. What is kept in
# git is each study's summary.csv and manifest.json (for the shipped settings
# here, for the tuned ones under tuned/): small, and the numbers the thesis
# quotes, with what produced them -- plus the per-tick records a figure draws
# (below), so every figure can be made from a clone, on any machine.
**/cells/
**/series/*

# The per-tick records figures.py reads (C.series), ~40 files, a few MB in git:
# fig01-03 (nominal), fig06 (balanced, circle), fig10's histogram (timing, the
# five repeats per controller), fig11 (constraint), fig14's time series
# (estimator, rectangle, SQP, seed 1). For both variants. The live run and the
# Jetson comparison keep a tick histogram (tick_hist.json) instead of their
# hundreds of per-tick records.
!nominal/series/*
!balanced/series/*
!timing/series/*
!constraint/series/*
!estimator/series/rectangle_*_sqp_s1.csv
!tuned/nominal/series/*
!tuned/balanced/series/*
!tuned/timing/series/*
!tuned/constraint/series/*
!tuned/estimator/series/rectangle_*_sqp_s1.csv

# Live trials (live/driver.py): ~1.7 MB each, hundreds per run. Git keeps each
# trial's parameter files, report.json and summary.csv, and what collect.py
# derives from the per-tick records for the figures (summary.csv, tick_hist.csv,
# plot_series/); the per-tick records and logs themselves stay on the machine
# that ran them. The pilot predates this and is tracked whole.
live/*/trials/*/series.csv
live/*/trials/*/series.csv.ticks.json
live/*/trials/*/trace_*.csv
live/*/trials/*/stack.log
live/*/failed/
live/*/.lock
live/*.log
!live/pilot/**
